<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ruleset name="Assinafy"
	xmlns="https://pmd.sourceforge.io/ruleset/2.0.0"
	xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
	xsi:schemaLocation="https://pmd.sourceforge.io/ruleset/2.0.0 https://pmd.sourceforge.io/ruleset_2_0_0.xsd">

	<description>
		Complexity and dead-code analysis for the Assinafy WordPress plugin.

		PHPMD ships rules written for general PHP, several of which contradict the WordPress
		coding standards that PHPCS enforces on the same files. Where the two disagree,
		WordPress wins — a plugin that ignores its platform's conventions fails review — so
		those rules are tuned here with the reason stated. Everything left measures something
		real: complexity, dead code, and constructs that hide bugs.

		Every deviation below is either a rule property narrowed to the exact construct that
		is unavoidable, or a whole-rule exclusion where no property can express the exception.
		Nothing in `src/` carries an inline `@SuppressWarnings`.
	</description>

	<rule ref="rulesets/cleancode.xml">
		<!--
			The reported call sites are the plugin's own static accessors and named
			constructors — `Settings::get()`, `Capabilities::add()`, `Route::url()`,
			`ApiException::fromResponse()` — plus WooCommerce's `FeaturesUtil`. WordPress has
			no container to resolve them through, and the dependencies that can be injected
			already are, through constructors. What is left is state that is global by
			definition: the site's options, its capabilities, and the SDK's own factories.
		-->
		<exclude name="StaticAccess"/>

		<!--
			Re-declared below with `ignore-global` on.
		-->
		<exclude name="MissingImport"/>
	</rule>

	<!--
		`\InvalidArgumentException` and `\RuntimeException` are written fully qualified at the
		throw site. Importing SPL's global-namespace classes into a plugin namespace buys
		nothing and hides which are PHP's and which are the plugin's. Any other unimported
		class — an SDK type, a WordPress class — is still reported.
	-->
	<rule ref="rulesets/cleancode.xml/MissingImport">
		<properties>
			<property name="ignore-global" value="true"/>
		</properties>
	</rule>

	<rule ref="rulesets/codesize.xml"/>

	<rule ref="rulesets/design.xml">
		<!--
			`exit` is how a WordPress request ends after a redirect or a streamed file, and
			omitting it lets execution continue into output the handler already replaced.
			`DownloadProxy` and the `admin_post_` handlers rely on it.
		-->
		<exclude name="ExitExpression"/>

		<!--
			Re-declared below with the composition root's own count as the maximum.
		-->
		<exclude name="CouplingBetweenObjects"/>
	</rule>

	<!--
		`Plugin` is the single wiring file: `register()` constructs the object graph and hands
		each piece to WordPress, so it names every class the plugin has. Its coupling is 20.
		Splitting it would only move the same edge list into a second file that `Plugin` then
		also depends on.

		The rule fires at or above its `maximum`, so 21 admits the wiring file's 20 and nothing
		above it. That is the composition root's own count, not a round number chosen to clear
		it: the next most coupled class is `WpHttpClient` at 11, so every class that is not the
		wiring file still clears the rule by a wide margin, and one more dependency in `Plugin`
		is reported.
	-->
	<rule ref="rulesets/design.xml/CouplingBetweenObjects">
		<properties>
			<property name="maximum" value="21"/>
		</properties>
	</rule>

	<rule ref="rulesets/unusedcode.xml">
		<!--
			WordPress and WP-CLI pass a fixed argument list to every callback. A WP-CLI command
			that reads only `$assoc_args` still has to declare the positional `$args` the
			dispatcher calls it with. The rule has no property to scope this to a signature.
		-->
		<exclude name="UnusedFormalParameter"/>

		<!--
			The rule resolves a private call only through `$this->` and `self::`. `Plugin`
			holds its instance in a static property and boots through it —
			`self::$instance->register()` — which the rule cannot follow, so it reports
			`register()` as dead code while it is the method that builds the entire object
			graph. It is provably wrong here, and it is the whole rule that is wrong: PHPMD
			offers no property or pattern to scope it to one call shape.

			Nothing is lost by dropping it. PHPStan reports an unused private method under
			`method.unused`, resolves the call through the static property correctly, and runs
			at level 8 over the same files on the same CI job. Confirmed against a class
			holding one private method reached through a static property and one reached by
			nothing: PHPStan flags the second and leaves the first alone.
		-->
		<exclude name="UnusedPrivateMethod"/>
	</rule>

	<rule ref="rulesets/naming.xml">
		<!--
			`$e` for a caught exception and `$id` for an identifier are clearer than the longer
			names this rule would otherwise force. Every other short name is still reported.
		-->
		<exclude name="ShortVariable"/>
	</rule>

	<rule ref="rulesets/naming.xml/ShortVariable">
		<properties>
			<property name="exceptions" value="e,id"/>
		</properties>
	</rule>

	<!--
		`controversial` is not included. It is `Superglobals` plus the camelCase rules.
		`Superglobals` forbids reading `$_POST` in a form handler or a REST callback — the exact
		places WordPress requires superglobals to be read, and where PHPCS's WordPress.Security
		sniffs already verify the nonce check, the capability check, `wp_unslash()` and the
		sanitiser. The camelCase rules are the direct opposite of the snake_case naming
		WordPress mandates and PHPCS enforces on these same files through WordPress-Extra.
		Neither describes a defect in this codebase.
	-->

</ruleset>
